\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Discussion}
\title{The field equations in three languages}
\date{2026-11-04}


\p{In the undergraduate course on electromagnetism the field equations arrive
in the language of vector calculus. With a charge density #{\rho} and a current
#{J}, and with the constants set to one, they read
##{\operatorname{div} E=\rho,\qquad \operatorname{div} B=0,\qquad
   \covwedge E=-\partial_t B,\qquad
   \covwedge B=\partial_t E+J.}
4 equations for two vector fields, with a potential #{A} that enters when one
wants to solve them. There the potential is primitive, and the fields are
##{E=-\nabla\varphi-\partial_t A,\qquad B=\covwedge A,}
with #{\varphi} the scalar potential. The metric sits in the operators: the
divergence, the curl and the cross product all know about lengths and angles.
Every object is continuous as well: #{\rho} is a density, and #{\partial_t}
presupposes a parameter along which to differentiate.}

\p{A course in mathematical physics rewrites the system in the language of
differential forms. The potential becomes a #{1}-form #{A} and the field
strength is locally its exterior derivative #{F=dA}. The exterior derivative
needs no metric, so #{dF=0} is a consequence of #{d\circ d=0} rather than an
equation to impose, and gauge transformations #{A\mapsto A+d\chi} leave #{F}
unchanged by construction, which is why gauge theory is written this way.
\citet{Example 28.5.5}{hassani2013mathematical} and
\citet{Box 28.5.6}{hassani2013mathematical} write #{dF=0}. The metric reappears
in the dual that the sourced equation requires,
##{d(F\psI^{-1})=4\pi(J\psI^{-1}),}
where #{J} is the current #{1}-form, and #{d} raises the degree of a form.
\citet{Box 28.5.14}{hassani2013mathematical} writes that equation.}

\p{Readers who have met geometric calculus will recognize the same system in the
unified language that Hestenes and Sobczyk proposed for mathematics and physics.
Relative to a time direction #{\gamma_0}, the electric and magnetic fields are
parts of one bivector: #{E} is a relative vector and #{\psI B} a relative bivector,
##{F=E+\psI B,}
where #{\psI} is the pseudoscalar of the algebra, the product of the 4 orthonormal
frame vectors, with #{\psI^2=-1}. \citet{(7.10)}{doran2003geometric} writes #{E}
as a relative vector and #{\psI B} as a relative bivector. The magnetic field
enters as the dual bivector #{\psI B}, and the pair forms one bivector rather than
the vector and pseudovector of vector calculus. The derivative is the vector
derivative
#{\nabla}: the derivative along each direction of a chosen frame, combined into a
single vector-valued operator by the geometric product with the frame vectors.
It acts by geometric multiplication, and a vector multiplied that way by a
multivector splits into two parts,
##{\nabla F=\nabla\cdot F+\nabla\wedge F,}
an inner product giving a divergence and an outer product giving an exterior part. With it
the 4 equations become #{\nabla F=J}, the divergence part carrying the sources
and the exterior part vanishing, so each original equation returns as a definite
grade. \citet{(7.14)}{doran2003geometric} writes that equation. Hestenes and
Sobczyk develop the calculus \citek{hestenes2012clifford}, Doran and Lasenby
carry it through classical electrodynamics
\citet{Section 7.1}{doran2003geometric}, and Macdonald treats them at textbook
level \citet{pp. 63--64}{macdonald2012vector}. Here
one algebra and one derivative serve both objects, and the metric is neither an
operator nor a dual: it is inside the geometric product, which is why the
language carries a chosen spacetime with it.}

\p{All three ways of writing these equations need a limit: in each, a derivative
compares values at points whose separation shrinks. Those points are not bare:
the continuum carries a metric, a topology and a smooth structure, and that is
what gives a separation, a direction and a gradient their meaning. On a lattice
only finitely many points remain, and a direction becomes a step from one site
to a neighbor. What is left of the gradient is the difference of the values at
the two ends of each step. A derivative of a product obeys the Leibniz rule; does
a difference of values obey it as well, or does something else take its place?
And which changes of the potential does a difference fail to see?}
